    "q-346680": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346680",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=1[x\\ge -1]\\cdot {x}^{2}+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $f(-3)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge -1]=1[-3\\ge -1]=0$ (since $-3\\ge -1$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =1[-3\\ge -1]\\cdot (-3)^{2}+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 9+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
