    "q-347433": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347433",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=1[x\\ge 2]\\cdot (x-2)^{2}+1[-1\\le x<2]\\cdot 1+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $g(-3)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 2]=1[-3\\ge 2]=0$ (since $-3\\ge 2$ is false)\n\n- $1[-1\\le x<2]=1[-1\\le -3<2]=0$ (since $-1\\le -3$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =1[-3\\ge 2]\\cdot (-5)^{2}+1[-1\\le -3<2]\\cdot 1+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ only the third term contributes to the result.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
